      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the length of the longest side of a similar triangle whose perimeter is 110 cm, given that the sides of the original triangle are 6 cm, 7 cm, and 9 cm.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the Perimeter of the Original Triangle\n\nFirst, we find the perimeter of the original triangle. The sides are given as 6 cm, 7 cm, and 9 cm.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Perimeter} = 6 + 7 + 9\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Perimeter} = 22 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Determine the Scale Factor\n\nNext, we need to find the scale factor between the original triangle and the similar triangle. The perimeter of the similar triangle is given as 110 cm. The scale factor \\( k \\) is the ratio of the perimeters of the two triangles.\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{\\text{Perimeter of similar triangle}}{\\text{Perimeter of original triangle}} = \\frac{110}{22}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nk = 5\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the Sides of the Similar Triangle\n\nTo find the lengths of the sides of the similar triangle, we multiply each side of the original triangle by the scale factor \\( k \\).\n\n\\[\n\\text{Side 1 of similar triangle} = 6 \\times 5 = 30 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Side 2 of similar triangle} = 7 \\times 5 = 35 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Side 3 of similar triangle} = 9 \\times 5 = 45 \\text{ cm}\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Identify the Longest Side\n\nThe longest side of the similar triangle is the third side, which is 45 cm.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{45}\n\\]",
