      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The given triangle has sides of 6 cm, 7 cm, and 9 cm. We first find the perimeter of the given triangle:\n\nPerimeter of given triangle \\(P = 6 + 7 + 9 = 22\\) cm\n\nThe problem is to find the length of the longest side of a similar triangle with a perimeter of 110 cm. Since the triangles are similar, the ratio of the corresponding sides is the same. We need to find this ratio first.\n\nThe formula for the ratio of the perimeters of two similar triangles is:\n\\[ \\text{Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Perimeter of similar triangle}}{\\text{Perimeter of given triangle}} = \\frac{110}{22} = 5 \\]\n\nThis means that each side of the similar triangle is 5 times the length of the corresponding side of the given triangle. The longest side of the original triangle is 9 cm. Therefore, the length of the longest side of the similar triangle is:\n\\[ 9 \\times 5 = 45 \\]\n\nThe length of the longest side of the similar triangle is \\(\\boxed{45}\\) cm.\n\nIf we are dealing with a more complex scenario where a similar triangle has its longest side measuring 45 cm instead, what would be the length of the longest side of the original triangle if it is also given that the original triangle's longest side is 'x' cm?\nTo find the length of the longest side of the original triangle when the longest side of the similar triangle is given and the longest side of the original triangle is 'x', we start by noting that the similarity ratio between the two triangles is the same. Let's denote the longest side of the original triangle as \\( x \\).\n\nThe perimeter of the similar triangle is given as 110 cm. Since the similarity ratio is the same for all corresponding sides, the ratio of the perimeters of the similar triangle and the original triangle is 5 (since \\( \\frac{110}{22} = 5 \\)).\n\nThis means that the longest side of the original triangle, which is \\( x \\), will be 5 times the longest side of the similar triangle, which is 45 cm. Therefore, we can set up the following equation:\n\\[ x = 5 \\times 45 = 225 \\]\n\nThus, the length of the longest side of the original triangle is \\(\\boxed{225}\\).\n\nGiven that the function \\( f(x) = a \\cos x + b \\sqrt[3]{x} + 4 \\) (where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are real constants), and \\( f(\\lg \\log_2 10) = 5 \\), determine the value of \\( f(\\lg \\lg 2) \\).\nTo determine the value of \\( f(\\lg \\lg 2) \\), we start by analyzing the given function \\( f(x) = a \\cos x + b \\sqrt[3]{x} + 4 \\). We know that \\( f(\\lg \\log_2 10) = 5 \\). We need to find \\( f(\\lg \\lg 2) \\).\n\nFirst, let's consider the properties of the function. Notice that if we replace \\( x \\) with \\(-x\\) in the function, we get:\n\\[ f(-x) = a \\cos(-x) + b \\sqrt[3]{-x} + 4 = a \\cos x - b \\sqrt[3]{x} + 4. \\]\nThus, the function \\( f(x) \\) is even because \\( f(-x) = f(x) \\).\n\nGiven that \\( f(\\lg \\log_2 10) = 5 \\), we can use the even nature of the function to find \\( f(\\lg \\lg 2) \\). Since \\( \\lg \\lg 2 = -\\lg \\log_2 10 \\), we have:\n\\[ f(\\lg \\lg 2) = f(-\\lg \\log_2 10) = f(\\lg \\log_2 10) = 5. \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( f(\\lg \\lg 2) \\) is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).\n\nThe negation of the proposition \"$\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x^2 - 2x + 1 \\leq 0$\" is (　　)\n\nA: $\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x^2 - 2x + 1 > 0$\n\nB: $\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x^2 - 2x + 1 > 0$\n\nC: $\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x^2 - 2x + 1 \\geq 0$\n\nD: $\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $x^2 - 2x + 1 < 0$\nTo find the negation of the proposition \"\\(\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\), \\(x^2 - 2x + 1 \\leq 0\\)\", we need to understand the structure of the statement and how to negate it.\n\nThe original proposition is an existential statement, which means it asserts the existence of at least one real number \\(x\\) for which the inequality \\(x^2 - 2x + 1 \\leq 0\\) holds. The negation of an existential statement is a universal statement that asserts the opposite for all elements in the domain. Therefore, the negation of \"\\(\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\), \\(x^2 - 2x + 1 \\leq 0\\)\" is \"\\(\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\), \\(x^2 - 2x + 1 > 0\\)\".\n\nLet's break it down step by step:\n\n1. Identify the original proposition: \"\\(\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\), \\(x^2 - 2x + 1 \\leq 0\\)\".\n2. Negate the existential quantifier: Replace \"\\(\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\)\" with \"\\(\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\)\".\n3. Negate the inequality: Replace \"\\(\\leq 0\\)\" with \"\\(> 0\\)\".\n\nSo, the negation of \"\\(\\exists x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\), \\(x^2 - 2x + 1 \\leq 0\\)\" is \"\\(\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}\\), \\(x^2 - 2x + 1 > 0\\)\".\n\nTherefore, the correct choice is \\(\\boxed{B}\\).",
